    title: 'The Anomalies of Economic Reason',
    passageText: `Classical economic theory rests on the assumption that individuals are rational agents who maximize expected utility — calculating the probable outcomes of available choices, weighting them by their probabilities, and selecting the option with the highest expected value. This model is elegant, tractable, and serves as the foundation of an enormous intellectual apparatus. Its central failing is that human beings frequently do not behave this way.

Kahneman and Tversky's research program, developed through the 1970s and 1980s, documented systematic deviations from rational choice. Their "prospect theory" showed that individuals weight losses more heavily than equivalent gains — the pain of losing fifty dollars exceeds the pleasure of gaining fifty dollars of equal objective value. This phenomenon, loss aversion, generates predictable biases: individuals hold losing investments too long (unwilling to realize a loss), accept unfavorable gambles to avoid certain losses, and respond asymmetrically to gain-framed versus loss-framed descriptions of identical options.

The framing effect — the finding that identical information presented in different frames produces different choices — is perhaps the most philosophically disturbing implication of behavioral economics. If I tell you that a medical treatment "has a 90% survival rate," you will likely endorse it; if I tell you the same treatment "has a 10% mortality rate," you may resist it. The objective information is identical; only the frame differs. For a rational agent, frame should be irrelevant; only the objective content of the choice should matter. The reliable frame-dependence of human choice reveals that what people are responding to is not the objective choice situation but a constructed mental representation of it.

Behavioral economists and their critics have debated what follows from these findings. One response — Sunstein and Thaler's "libertarian paternalism" — proposes using what is known about cognitive biases to "nudge" people toward choices that serve their own long-term interests, by designing choice architectures that exploit the same cognitive mechanisms that cause the biases in the first place. Make healthy food the default cafeteria option; automatically enroll employees in retirement savings plans with an opt-out rather than opt-in requirement. If people have biased preferences, one can design environments that counteract the biases without restricting freedom of choice.

Critics from both the left and the libertarian right have objected to nudge theory. From the left: nudges are technocratic interventions that treat the symptoms rather than the structural causes of poor decision-making (poverty, stress, inadequate education). From the right: even "choice-preserving" nudges involve government manipulation of private decision-making — the choice architect's judgments about what is in people's interests are paternalistic regardless of how the choice structure is described.`,
