    title: 'The Novel as Social Mirror',
    passageText: `Georg Lukács argued in his influential study of the novel that the form represents the "epic of a world abandoned by God." Where the ancient epic expressed a world unified by immanent meaning — heroes moved through a cosmos in which their actions were cosmically legible — the novel emerged in a historical moment when such unity had dissolved. The protagonist of the modern novel is a problematic individual: inwardly complex, defined by a gap between self and world, seeking a meaning that the objective social world can no longer provide. The novel's formal structure — its narrative interiority, its ironic relation to its own content — reflects this metaphysical homelessness.

Ian Watt's account of the novel's rise reached similar destinations by different routes. For Watt, the novel emerged from and reflected the social conditions of early modern capitalism: the rise of an individualist worldview, the expansion of print culture, the emergence of a literate middle class with leisure for private reading and interest in psychological interiority. The novel's formal innovations — the illusion of historical particularity, the individualized rather than generic characters, the "formal realism" that insisted on grounding fiction in concrete, dateable social life — were conditioned by the specific social structure of eighteenth-century England.

Both Lukács and Watt have been criticized for their apparent reductionism — the assumption that literary form can be directly read off from social conditions, as if novels were merely symptoms of their historical moment rather than active participants in shaping consciousness and social life. Franco Moretti's quantitative approach to literary history added a further complication: examining publication patterns across national traditions, he found that the novel's rise was not uniform across Europe. The form spread unevenly, hybridized with local traditions, and produced wildly different aesthetic outcomes in different national contexts, suggesting that the relationship between social conditions and literary form is neither simple nor deterministic.

The debate about the novel's social role has intensified in the age of the internet, when many predicted the novel's death as a mass cultural form. Those predictions have proven partly wrong — the total number of novels published each year has increased — and partly right — the novel's share of cultural attention has declined relative to streaming video, social media, and gaming. Whether the novel retains its capacity to perform the distinctive social and epistemic functions that Lukács and Watt attributed to it — cultivating interiority, representing social totality, providing a space for sustained, linear, private reflection — is a genuinely open question.`,
